      <h2>For men who are done <i>keeping score.</i></h2>
      <p class="lead">Biblical coaching is not therapy and not a pep talk. It is one older man opening Scripture with a younger one, asking hard questions, and staying in the room. I've been forgiven of a great deal. That is the only credential that matters here.</p>
      <p class="verse">Iron sharpens iron, and one man sharpens another.<small>Proverbs 27:17</small></p>
    </div>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><b>1</b><div><h4>A first conversation</h4><p>Forty-five minutes, no charge. Where you are, what you're carrying, whether this is a fit.</p></div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>2</b><div><h4>Scripture, weekly</h4><p>A passage, a question, an honest hour. Forgiveness, identity, work, marriage, fathering, starting over.</p></div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>3</b><div><h4>Walk it out</h4><p>Between sessions you do the thing you said you'd do. I check. That's the mentoring part.</p></div></div>
